@ ../cmd/root.go
Phase 1 stands up the CLI scaffolding, not just the file parser: a
`cobra` root command named `tour`. Every later phase (the viewer,
`agent-instructions`, `version`) adds a command to this same tree
rather than standing up its own — this is the one place that happens.
---
@ ../tourfile/parse.go:33
The whole grammar comes down to one regex: `@ file:line`, with the
`:line` group optional. When it's omitted, the slide defaults to line
1 — useful for a slide that's about a whole file rather than one spot
in it.
---
@ ../tourfile/parse.go:39
`Parse` scans the file in blocks separated by `---` lines. Each block
is handed to `parseBlock` independently, and parsing never stops at
the first bad block — it just moves on to the next `---` and keeps
going, so a single `tour parse` run can report every structural
problem in the file at once instead of one-at-a-time as an author
fixes and reruns.
---
@ ../tourfile/parse.go:76
`parseBlock` finds the first non-blank line of the block, requires it
to match the `@ file:line` shape, and treats everything after it as
narration text (`strings.TrimSpace` on the joined remainder — trims
the outer whitespace but leaves internal blank lines and paragraph
breaks alone).
---
@ ../tourfile/validate.go:25
`ValidateLocations` is deliberately separate from `Parse` — it's the
part that needs the filesystem. Every slide's file is resolved
relative to `tourDir` (the tour file's own directory), never the
process's working directory, so a tour stays correct no matter where
`tour` is invoked from.
---
@ ../cmd/parse.go:29
This is where the two pieces come together: `runParse` calls
`tourfile.Parse`, then `tourfile.ValidateLocations` against the tour
file's own directory, then prints a report — slide count, each
slide's location exactly as the author wrote it, and every error
found. A non-zero return value here is what turns into `tour parse`'s
exit code, for CI and pre-commit use.
---
@ ../cmd/parse.go:65
The narration preview shown for each slide is truncated by rune, not
by byte, and only to the first line — the two details that make a
"first line, `...` if there's more" summary actually correct for
arbitrary multi-paragraph UTF-8 narration instead of just usually
correct.
---
@ ../main.go:10
`main` is the only place a `tourfile`/`cmd` error becomes a process
exit code. Everything below this line already reported what went
wrong to stdout; this just decides whether the process exits 0 or 1.
---
@ ../cmd/parse_test.go:33
Every scenario in `fts/tour-parse.ft` has exactly one test like this
one, tagged with its `@ft:<id>` immediately above the function. `ft
sync` found this tag and linked `TestParse_WellFormedTour` to
scenario 1 automatically — nothing hand-registered the connection.
